Feature: SSH connections, named connections and command output checks

  Background: Connect to the bootstrap machine
    Given I open a ssh connection to '${SSH}' with user 'root' and password 'stratio'

  Scenario: The port can be given explicitly
    Given I open a ssh connection to '${SSH}' in port '22' with user 'root' and password 'stratio'
    When I run 'echo reachable' in the ssh connection with exit status '0'
    Then the command output contains 'reachable'

  Scenario: Several connections can be kept open under their own ids
    Given I open a ssh connection to '${SSH}' with user 'root' and password 'stratio' and saved with id 'first'
    And I open a ssh connection to '${SSH}' with user 'root' and password 'stratio' and saved with id 'second'
    When I run 'echo from-first' in the ssh connection with id 'first' with exit status '0'
    Then the command output contains 'from-first'
    When I run 'echo from-second' in the ssh connection with id 'second' with exit status '0'
    Then the command output contains 'from-second'

  Scenario: The active connection can be switched and closed by id
    Given I open a ssh connection to '${SSH}' with user 'root' and password 'stratio' and saved with id 'alpha'
    And I open a ssh connection to '${SSH}' with user 'root' and password 'stratio' and saved with id 'beta'
    When I put remote SSH connection with id 'alpha' as active
    And I run 'echo active-is-alpha' in the ssh connection with exit status '0'
    Then the command output contains 'active-is-alpha'
    When I close SSH connection with id 'beta'
    And I run 'echo alpha-still-open' in the ssh connection with id 'alpha' with exit status '0'
    Then the command output contains 'alpha-still-open'
    And I close SSH connection with id 'alpha'

  Scenario: Checking the output against a regular expression
    When I run 'echo bdt-2026' in the ssh connection
    Then the command output matches regex '.*bdt-\d{4}.*'

  Scenario: A command that prints nothing has an empty output
    When I run 'true' in the ssh connection
    Then the command output is empty

  Scenario: The exit status can be asserted on its own
    When I run 'exit 7' in the ssh connection with exit status '7'
    Then the command exit status is '7'

  Scenario: A remote command can be given a timeout
    When I run 'echo quick' in the ssh connection with exit status '0' with a timeout of '30' seconds
    Then the command output contains 'quick'

  Scenario: A local command can be given a timeout
    When I run 'echo local-quick' locally with exit status '0' and save the value in environment variable 'localQuick' with a timeout of '30' seconds
    Then '!{localQuick}' contains 'local-quick'

  Scenario: Polling a remote command until its output shows up
    Then in less than '20' seconds, checking each '2' seconds, the command output 'echo polled' contains 'polled' with exit status '0'
    And in less than '20' seconds, checking each '2' seconds, the command output 'echo polled' not contains 'absent'

  Scenario: Polling a remote command until its output matches exactly
    Then in less than '20' seconds, checking each '2' seconds, the command output 'echo -n exact' is equal to 'exact' with exit status '0'
    And in less than '20' seconds, checking each '2' seconds, the command output 'echo -n exact' is not equal to 'different'

  Scenario: Polling a local command until its output shows up
    Then in less than '20' seconds, checking each '2' seconds, the local command output 'echo localpolled' contains 'localpolled' with exit status '0'
    And in less than '20' seconds, checking each '2' seconds, the local command output 'echo localpolled' not contains 'absent'

  Scenario: Polling a local command until its output matches exactly
    Then in less than '20' seconds, checking each '2' seconds, the local command output 'echo -n localexact' is equal to 'localexact' with exit status '0'

  Scenario: A connection can authenticate with a key instead of a password
    # The key pair is generated by the runner and the public half installed here, so nothing has
    # to be committed and the pem branch -- 2547 uses across qa-unified-tests -- is really run.
    Given I run 'mkdir -p /root/.ssh && chmod 700 /root/.ssh' in the ssh connection with exit status '0'
    And I run 'echo "${SSH_PUBLIC_KEY}" > /root/.ssh/authorized_keys && chmod 600 /root/.ssh/authorized_keys' in the ssh connection with exit status '0'
    When I open a ssh connection to '${SSH}' with user 'root' using pem file '${SSH_PEM_FILE}'
    And I run 'echo authenticated-with-a-key' in the ssh connection with exit status '0'
    Then the command output contains 'authenticated-with-a-key'

  Scenario: A key-based connection can also be named and copy files
    Given I run 'mkdir -p /root/.ssh && chmod 700 /root/.ssh' in the ssh connection with exit status '0'
    And I run 'echo "${SSH_PUBLIC_KEY}" > /root/.ssh/authorized_keys && chmod 600 /root/.ssh/authorized_keys' in the ssh connection with exit status '0'
    When I open a ssh connection to '${SSH}' in port '22' with user 'root' using pem file '${SSH_PEM_FILE}' and saved with id 'bykey'
    And I run 'rm -f /tmp/byKeyCopy.conf /tmp/exampleJSON.conf' in the ssh connection with exit status '0'
    And I outbound copy 'src/test/resources/exampleJSON.conf' through a ssh connection to '/tmp/byKeyCopy.conf'
    And I run 'test -s /tmp/byKeyCopy.conf' in the ssh connection with id 'bykey' with exit status '0'
    # scp drops the file in the destination directory under its local name and a second command moves
    # it to the requested path. Both used to be disconnected without waiting for the remote end, so
    # the copy could report success with nothing at the destination. Asserting the staging name is
    # gone pins the move down; asserting the content pins the transfer itself.
    And I run 'test ! -e /tmp/exampleJSON.conf' in the ssh connection with id 'bykey' with exit status '0'
    And I run 'cat /tmp/byKeyCopy.conf' in the ssh connection with id 'bykey' with exit status '0'
    And the command output contains 'example glossary'
    Then I close SSH connection with id 'bykey'
